  {
   "id": "noteIntro",
   "type": "NoteNode",
   "x": 0,
   "y": 66,
   "init": {
    "label": "A tiny transformer",
    "color": "violet",
    "collapsed": false,
    "width": 760,
    "height": 520,
    "body": "# A tiny transformer\n\nThis is the forward pass of a language model, the same arithmetic as GPT at toy size: one layer, one attention head, four dimensions and five words. Every number sits on a card, so you can change any weight and watch the prediction move.\n\n**Embed.** Each word of the prompt *the cat sat on* is a row: its token embedding plus its position's.\n\n**Attend.** Each word asks with its query and answers with its key. A query times a key, over √4, scores how much one word should look at another; the mask hides every word that comes after, and a softmax turns each row into weights that sum to 1. The weights mix the values, and the mix is added back to X. Here *sat* and *on* put nearly all their attention on *cat*.\n\n**Think.** A two-layer network with a ReLU works on each word by itself, and its answer is added back too.\n\n**Predict.** The unembedding scores every word of the vocabulary, and a softmax makes the scores probabilities. Each position predicts the word after it: *the* expects *cat* or *mat*, *cat* expects *sat*, *sat* expects *on*, and *on* expects *the*.\n\nThe weights here were set by hand to tell that story; a real model learns billions of them from text. Try W_K: change its 3 to 0, and the attention on *cat* spreads out.",
    "fieldTypes": {}
   }
  },
